From dc2e8f2b797e7e44409567a68135e531843bf6bc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 15:32:33 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 015/115] misc: easel-mipi: add Easel MIPI bridge driver

On the Google Pixel 2 / 2 XL both camera sensors' CSI lanes terminate
on Easel (Google Pixel Visual Core / Intel Monette Hill) before being
retransmitted to the SoC's CSIPHY0/CSIPHY1. Until Easel's MIPI mux is
put in bypass, the SoC's CSIPHYs receive nothing at all.

This driver claims the PCI endpoint so the core assigns its BARs and
turns on memory decoding -- without a driver, COMMAND stays 0x0000,
every BAR reads back 0, and any MMIO access to it is a transaction to
nowhere that hangs the fabric. It then retargets BAR2's inbound iATU
window (8 MB, but the peripherals live at 0x04000000) and programs
the RX->TX bypass mux for each configured link.

Exports easel_mipi_kick(), used by the imx179/imx362 sensor drivers:
the msm8998 CSIPHY clock-lane detector only arms on an LP->HS
transition seen after the CSIPHY is configured, and Easel's TX clock
has been running continuously HS since module load, so a CSIPHY
configured mid-session never sees one. A short LP pulse on the TX
clock lane, issued from the sensor's s_stream(), makes every stream
attempt equivalent to one immediately after Easel's own probe.

Built as a module rather than built-in on purpose: a built-in driver
that touches an unresponsive Easel would hang the boot, where a
loadable module fails safely instead.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
